<!-- FAQ Section -->
<section class="faq">
    <hgroup class="flexy">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)</h2>
        <p>Common questions about Progressive Web Applications</p>
    </hgroup>
    <div class="container">
        <details name="faq-item">
            <summary>What exactly is a Progressive Web App?</summary>
            <p>A Progressive Web App (PWA) is a type of application built using web technologies (HTML, CSS, and JavaScript), but offers an experience similar to native mobile apps. PWAs are progressive, responsive, connectivity independent, app-like, fresh, safe, discoverable, re-engageable, installable, and linkable.</p>
        </details>
        <details name="faq-item">
            <summary>How do PWAs work offline?</summary>
            <p>PWAs use service workers, which are JavaScript files that run separately from the main browser thread, to intercept network requests and cache resources. This allows the app to load previously visited pages and assets even when there's no internet connection.</p>
        </details>
        <details name="faq-item">
            <summary>Are PWAs supported on all browsers?</summary>
            <p>Most modern browsers support PWA features, including Chrome, Firefox, Edge, Safari, and Opera. While some features may have varying levels of support across browsers, we build PWAs with progressive enhancement in mind, ensuring they work for all users while providing enhanced experiences where supported.</p>
        </details>
        <details name="faq-item">
            <summary>How long does it take to develop a PWA?</summary>
            <p>Development time varies based on complexity, features, and design requirements. A basic PWA might take 4-6 weeks, while more complex applications with extensive functionality could take 3-6 months. We'll provide a detailed timeline during our initial consultation based on your specific needs.</p>
        </details>
        <details name="faq-item">
            <summary>Can I convert my existing website to a PWA?</summary>
            <p>Yes, most existing websites can be converted to PWAs. The process typically involves adding a service worker, creating a web app manifest, ensuring the site is served over HTTPS, and optimizing for performance. We can assess your current website and recommend the best approach for conversion.</p>
        </details>
    </div>
</section>

